baseline.solution:
**Solution Explanation**

For every given point we know its Cartesian coordinates  
`(x , y)` – all coordinates are integers.  
Among all pairs of points we have to output the largest possible Euclidean
distance.

The direct way – checking all `O(n²)` pairs – is far too slow for the
limits of the task (up to `2·10⁵` points).  
The classical geometric trick is to use the *convex hull* of the set of
points and the *rotating‑calipers* method.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 1.  Geometry facts

* The farthest pair of points of a set lies on its convex hull.
  (If a point is inside the hull, moving it to the hull can only increase
  the distance to some other point.)

* For a convex polygon the farthest pair can be found in linear time
  with the rotating‑calipers algorithm.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 2.  Algorithm

```
read all points
if n == 1          → answer = 0
else
    build convex hull (Monotone chain, O(n log n))
    if hull has only 2 points
           answer = squared distance of the two hull points
    else
           rotating calipers on the hull
           answer = maximum squared distance found
output "Final Answer: " + answer
```

*Convex hull*  
The monotone chain algorithm sorts the points by `(x , y)` and builds the
lower and upper hulls.  
While adding a new point we remove the last point of the current hull
as long as the last three points do **not** make a left turn
(`cross ≤ 0`).  
The resulting hull is counter‑clockwise and contains no duplicate
endpoints.

*Rotating calipers*  
Let the hull contain `m` vertices `p[0] … p[m‑1]` (counter‑clockwise).
For every edge `p[i] → p[i+1]` we keep an index `j` that is the farthest
vertex from this edge.  
While the area of the triangle `p[i] , p[i+1] , p[(j+1)%m]` is larger
than the area of `p[i] , p[i+1] , p[j]` we advance `j`.  
During the scan we update the answer with the distances
`|p[i] – p[j]|` and `|p[i+1] – p[j]|`.  
The whole procedure is `O(m)`.

All distances are computed as squared Euclidean distances
(`dx*dx + dy*dy`) – this keeps everything integer and avoids
floating point errors.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 3.  Correctness Proof  

We prove that the algorithm outputs the maximum possible distance
between two points.

---

##### Lemma 1  
For any set of points the farthest pair of points lies on the convex
hull of the set.

**Proof.**

Assume a farthest pair `(a , b)` and suppose `a` is not on the hull.
Then `a` lies strictly inside the convex hull, i.e. it can be written
as a convex combination of hull vertices.  
Moving `a` to the hull along the line segment towards the hull
cannot decrease its distance to `b` (the segment is a straight line).
Thus a pair with at least one hull point is at least as far apart.
∎



##### Lemma 2  
During the rotating‑calipers scan, for every edge `p[i]p[i+1]` the index
`j` is the vertex that maximizes the distance to this edge.

**Proof.**

The algorithm starts with `j = 1`.  
For a fixed edge `p[i]p[i+1]` the area of the triangle
`p[i]p[i+1]p[j]` is proportional to the distance from `p[j]` to the
line through the edge.  
While the area increases when moving `j` to `j+1`, the distance to the
edge increases.  
When the area stops increasing, the next vertex would give a smaller
distance, so the current `j` is the farthest vertex from the edge.
∎



##### Lemma 3  
For every pair of vertices `(u , v)` of the convex hull, the algorithm
examines this pair (or a pair with the same distance) during the scan.

**Proof.**

Consider the edge of the hull that is *antipodal* to the pair `(u , v)`,
i.e. the edge for which the perpendicular from `v` to the edge is
maximal.  
When the scan processes this edge, by Lemma&nbsp;2 the index `j` will be
at a vertex that is farthest from the edge – that vertex is either `v`
or a vertex with the same distance to the edge.  
The algorithm updates the answer with the distances from `u` and from
the next vertex of the edge to `j`.  
Thus the distance `|u‑v|` (or an equal one) is considered.
∎



##### Lemma 4  
The value stored in `answer` after the scan equals the maximum squared
distance between any two hull vertices.

**Proof.**

From Lemma&nbsp;3 every pair of hull vertices is examined, and the
algorithm keeps the maximum of all examined distances.  
Therefore the final value is the maximum squared distance among all
hull vertices. ∎



##### Theorem  
The algorithm outputs the maximum possible Euclidean distance between
any two input points.

**Proof.**

By Lemma&nbsp;1 the farthest pair of input points is a pair of hull
vertices.  
By Lemma&nbsp;4 the algorithm finds the maximum squared distance among
all hull vertices.  
Consequently the algorithm outputs the squared distance of the farthest
pair of input points, which is the required answer. ∎



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 4.  Complexity Analysis

*Sorting for the hull*: `O(n log n)`  
*Building the hull*: `O(n)`  
*Rotating calipers*: `O(m)` where `m ≤ n`  
Total time: `O(n log n)`  
Memory usage: `O(n)` for storing the points and the hull.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 5.  Reference Implementation  (C++17)

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct Point {
    long long x, y;
    Point() {}
    Point(long long _x, long long _y) : x(_x), y(_y) {}
    Point operator-(const Point& other) const {
        return Point(x - other.x, y - other.y);
    }
};

long long cross(const Point& a, const Point& b) {
    return a.x * b.y - a.y * b.x;
}

long long dist2(const Point& a, const Point& b) {
    long long dx = a.x - b.x;
    long long dy = a.y - b.y;
    return dx * dx + dy * dy;
}

/* Monotone chain convex hull, returns vertices in counter‑clockwise order */
vector<Point> convexHull(vector<Point> pts) {
    int n = pts.size();
    if (n <= 1) return pts;
    sort(pts.begin(), pts.end(),
         [](const Point& a, const Point& b) {
             if (a.x != b.x) return a.x < b.x;
             return a.y < b.y;
         });

    vector<Point> lower, upper;
    for (const auto& p : pts) {
        while (lower.size() >= 2 &&
               cross(lower[lower.size() - 1] - lower[lower.size() - 2],
                     p - lower[lower.size() - 1]) <= 0)
            lower.pop_back();
        lower.push_back(p);
    }
    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
        const auto& p = pts[i];
        while (upper.size() >= 2 &&
               cross(upper[upper.size() - 1] - upper[upper.size() - 2],
                     p - upper[upper.size() - 1]) <= 0)
            upper.pop_back();
        upper.push_back(p);
    }
    // Concatenate lower and upper, removing duplicate endpoints
    lower.pop_back();
    upper.pop_back();
    lower.insert(lower.end(), upper.begin(), upper.end());
    return lower;
}

/* Rotating calipers to find farthest pair on a convex polygon */
long long farthestPair(const vector<Point>& hull) {
    int m = hull.size();
    if (m == 1) return 0;
    if (m == 2) return dist2(hull[0], hull[1]);

    long long best = 0;
    int j = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        int ni = (i + 1) % m;
        // Move j while area increases
        while (abs(cross(hull[ni] - hull[i], hull[(j + 1) % m] - hull[i])) >
               abs(cross(hull[ni] - hull[i], hull[j] - hull[i]))) {
            j = (j + 1) % m;
        }
        best = max(best, dist2(hull[i], hull[j]));
        best = max(best, dist2(hull[ni], hull[j]));
    }
    return best;
}

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    if (!(cin >> n)) return 0;
    vector<Point> pts(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        long long x, y;
        cin >> x >> y;
        pts[i] = Point(x, y);
    }

    vector<Point> hull = convexHull(pts);
    long long answer = farthestPair(hull);

    cout << "Final Answer: " << answer << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

The program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and
conforms to the required output format.

